<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berthold IV, Duke of Merania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">House of Andechs</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berthold IV (d. 1204), of the Bavarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">counts of Andechs</a>, was Duke of Merania from 1188, a title linked to lands on the Adriatic coast, and Margrave of Istria. He held wide lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His children married into royal and princely houses: Agnes became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Gertrude queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Hedwig duchess of Silesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-duke-of-merania.jpg" alt="Berthold IV, Duke of Merania" width="281" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berthold_IV_of_Merania_(Hedwig_Codex).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berthold was born around 1159, the son of Count Berthold III of Andechs and Hedwig of Wittelsbach. His father, a loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, had received the March of Istria on the death of the Sponheim margrave Engelbert III. The younger Berthold first appears in 1170, is named Count of Andechs in a deed of 1172 and was co-ruler of the march by 1175. He also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, where he counted as Berthold II. About 1180 he married Agnes of Rochlitz, daughter of Margrave Dedi III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Barbarossa deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, Berthold's maternal uncle Otto of Wittelsbach received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Berthold took the ducal title of Merania, named for the Kvarner coast his ancestors had conquered in the 1060s. Merania covered the same area as the old Istrian march and was no separate entity, but the title made the Andechs princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1186 he went with Henry VI to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for his marriage to Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> he was standard-bearer of the imperial army, leading the crusaders with Frederick VI, Duke of Swabia, at the Battle of Philomelion in 1190.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> it was agreed at Niš in 1189 that a daughter of his would marry Tohu, son of Prince Miroslav of Zahumlje, though the match seems never to have taken place. After Henry VI's death in 1197 he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> against Otto IV, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> supported. The house then held lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> to the Adriatic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Berthold died on 12 August 1204 and was buried at the family monastery of Dießen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> succeeded him as duke and became Count palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>; Henry II took Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>; Ekbert became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> and Berthold Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>. His daughter Hedwig, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-bearded">Henry I the Bearded</a> of Silesia, was canonised in 1267.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berthold of Merania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Berthold IV</b>'s daughters married widely: Agnes became Queen of France, Gertrude Queen of Hungary and mother of Saint Elizabeth, and Hedwig Duchess of Silesia, later Saint Hedwig.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-merania">Duchy of Merania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Dießen am Ammersee, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.95,11.1&amp;year=1194" aria-label="Dießen am Ammersee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 485 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Berthold IV, Duke of Merania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 755h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 755h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.95,11.1&amp;year=1194">What was here in 1194? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
